that said i want to explain the methodology---the best
way i can.  this is partially why i bought a new mac
with more than twice the money that my parents would
have given me to buy an iPhone 17-something with more
than 256G of NAND (with some indefinite icloud storage)
because i was always logged out of weixin and imessage
due to forgetting to charge and uninstalling apps due to
my photos and videos filling up my phone.  shortly after
setting it up from the previous ebay owner's factory
reset (apple discontinued the M4 in august (and the 64G
M4 Pro earlier in may) so i had to take the hard after-
market route >n>) it was 15.3.2 (24D81).  i upgraded to
the latest security patch, 15.8.1 (24H32) (though this
had to be done on the command-line as apple is very, VERY
eager to get me to hop on the macOS/iOS 27 bandwagon.)
and this is basically from me opening the Notes.app and
closing it, then giving Terminal.app Full Disk Access
(this is a necessary step; you are not above macOS even
if you become root) yanking out the entirety of

    ~/Library/Group Containers/group.com.apple.notes/

with rsync -r.  Change owner to me, make them writable
(i forgot if they were already writable, but i did it
explicitly anyways), open with sqlite3 (which comes with
bundled macOS), run "VACUUM INTO <NoteStore.sqlite under
a different path or some random name i will mv later>",
and ".quit".  (remember to quote your path of course.)

i think Claude did something similar but my method
described above produces byte-identical files anyways.
and yes, this is all to not have to deal with unflushed
write-ahead logs (WALs) and journal, because git-annex
clears the write bit on annexed files so doing so on a
database with all those additional artifacts might need
locking will be a nightmare.  (i may be horrible wrong
on this but well... it still holds that 2 more files
is three times as much git-annex metadata to track!)
